=== The "Wall Yeller" Incident ===
Early into the season, [[Cody Calafiore|Cody]] and [[Nicole Franzel|Nicole F]] became the dominant members of [[The Committee]], the majority alliance that controlled the entirety of the season. The alliance was controversial amongst fans as many felt they had made the season boring and predictable, with others believing that the alliance was organised through preseason pregaming. During Week 4, a fan, dubbed by the community as "The Wall Yeller," was heard on the 24/7 live feeds shouting "Nicole and Cody are playing everyone!" Afterwards, the feeds went down and did not return for another two hours.<ref>https://www.oprahdaily.com/entertainment/tv-movies/a33850089/wall-yeller-big-brother-all-stars-spoilers/</ref>

=== Contestants Mocking Ian's Autism ===
During the early days of the season, [[Ian Terry|Ian]] had revealed on the live feeds that he was diagnosed with Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) some time after [[Big Brother 14 (US)|Big Brother 14]].<ref>https://www.oprahdaily.com/entertainment/tv-movies/a33562318/big-brother-ian-terry-kaysar-ridha-autism/</ref> Many people with ASD are known to "stim" which refers to an act of repetitive movement used to soothe or help an individual deal with stress, which can include movements like rocking, pacing back and forth or repeating certain words and phrases. Later in the season, [[Daniele Briones|Dani]] was caught on live feeds talking to Nicole F, where she is heard talking about Ian, stating "I can't even look at him sometimes because his constant movement stresses me out," in reference to his rocking stim. She then asks Nicole F if she feels the same way, to which the latter agrees. In another clip she also accused him of using his ASD as a strategy. Later that day, [[Memphis Garrett|Memphis]]  brings up Ian in a conversation with Dani, Nicole F and [[Christmas Abbott|Christmas]], where he describes Ian as the "red rum" of the house (a reference to [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Shining_(film) The Shining]) and claims that he might have nightmares of Ian rocking at the end of his bed.<ref>https://www.tmz.com/2020/09/10/big-brother-houseguest-making-fun-ian-terry-autistic-autism-mocking-bullying/</ref> Numerous fans were upset by these clips, claiming that the contestants were bullying Ian, with some former Big Brother houseguests calling out the behaviour and denouncing production for not stepping in.<ref>https://x.com/JanellePierzina/status/1303895860015755265</ref> <ref>https://x.com/AndyHerren/status/1303921672001597443</ref> Ian later revealed that Dani had apologised to him in the Jury House and Nicole F had apologised to him sometime after the season.<ref>[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V_Gppqi6ZNk&t=3087s https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V_Gppqi6ZNk]</ref>

==Trivia==
[[File:BB22 Cast Picture.jpg|200px|thumb|right|A photo of the cast in full body, posted by [[Janelle Pierzina|Janelle]] in December 2023.]]
* With their 22nd season, the ''[[Big Brother U.S. (franchise)|Big Brother U.S.]]'' English speaking edition became the first country to air 22 civilian seasons of the show.
* With, ''[[Big Brother: After Dark]]'' not airing during the season, it will mark the first time since ''[[Big Brother 7 (US)]]'' that ''After Dark'' will not air alongside the main series.
** Coincidentally, season 7 was the first All-Star season.
* Every civilian season from ''[[Big Brother 6 (US)]]'' to ''[[Big Brother 21 (US)]]'' is represented on this cast, with the exception of [[Big Brother 9 (US)|seasons 9]], [[Big Brother 15 (US)|15]] and [[Big Brother: Over The Top|OTT]].
** However, no houseguests who debuted on [[Big Brother 13 (US)|seasons 13]] or [[Big Brother 18 (US)|18]] appear on this season. The three houseguests representing those seasons debuted on [[Big Brother 8 (US)|seasons 8]], [[Big Brother 16 (US)|16]] and [[Big Brother 17 (US)|17]] respectively.
* This is the first season since ''Big Brother 16 (US)'' where the first evictee was a woman.
** Coincidentally, on both seasons, the first evictee was evicted by a unanimous vote and the entire pre-jury had the same gender ratio: four women and one man.
** The first four women evicted this season ([[Keesha Smith|Keesha]], [[Nicole Anthony|Nicole A.]], [[Janelle Pierzina|Janelle]] and [[Bayleigh Dayton|Bayleigh]]) were all in the same entry group on Day 1.
* This season is tied with [[Big Brother 23 (US)]] for the longest time before a woman won an HOH or a POV, with the first HOH or POV win by a female occurring on Week 5.
* This is the first time since ''Big Brother 18 (US)'' that none of the pre-jury houseguests won an HOH or a POV.
* This is the first season since ''[[Big Brother 11 (US)]]'' to not feature a [[Double Eviction]]. Instead, this season had a [[Triple Eviction]], which was initially presented as a Double before "surprising" the houseguests with the third.
* This season has the second least instances the veto has been used, with only 2 instances. The season with the veto used the least times is ''[[Big Brother 3 (US)]],'' with only 1 instance.
** Coincidentally, both times it was used it was used on [[Kevin Campbell]].
* This is the first season since ''[[Big Brother 14 (US)]]'' to not feature a [[Previously Evicted Contestant]].
* This is the second season to not feature a successful [[backdoor]] since it was invented in ''[[Big Brother 5 (US)]]'', following ''[[Celebrity Big Brother 1 (US)]]''.
* This season is tied with ''Big Brother 14 (US)'' and ''Big Brother 21 (US)'' for the most amount of consecutive HOHs to also win the POV with three.
** This was accomplished by [[Tyler Crispen|Tyler]], [[Cody Calafiore|Cody]] and [[Nicole Franzel|Nicole F]] during Day 58 (Round 2), Week 9 & Week 10.
* There were four players who were playing for their third time this season (Kaysar, [[Dani Briones|Dani]], Nicole F and [[Da'Vonne Rogers|Da'Vonne]]). The three who were evicted before finale night were all evicted exactly three placements below the other: Kaysar placed 13th, Da'Vonne placed 10th and Dani placed 7th.
* The Final Two houseguests of the season, Cody and [[Enzo Palumbo|Enzo]], are both from the state of New Jersey.
* This is the sixth season where neither of the finalists had any eviction votes cast against them, following ''[[Big Brother 4 (US)]]'', ''Big Brother 14 (US)'', ''Big Brother 15 (US)'', ''Big Brother 16 (US)'' and ''Big Brother: Over The Top''.
* This is the first civilian season to not feature any showmances since ''Big Brother 1 (US)'', 20 years prior.
** However, houseguests [[Memphis Garrett|Memphis]] and [[Christmas Abbott|Christmas]] began a relationship after the season.

